1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:04,100
Hi, I'm Aishwarya from Novexa.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,800
I'm building a startup on AI security.

3
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:10,100
For this hackathon, I have built

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:15,300
an agentic purple teaming system where you can plug in your multi-agent system

5
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:19,000
in order to make it entirely secure.

6
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:23,500
So let's start with the demo.

7
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:29,000
Our purple teaming platform has five parts.

8
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:30,300
First is discovery.

9
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:35,800
It scans the entire code base to discover all the tools and agents.

10
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:38,500
Next is the attack, performed by the red teaming agent,

11
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:40,800
which continuously sends prompts to get sensitive information

12
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:43,900
or to perform a dangerous modification.

13
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,200
Next is the patch, performed by the blue team,

14
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:51,400
to apply the pre-written code-level patches chosen from the catalog.

15
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:54,600
It also comes with a runtime detector,

16
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:57,300
which detects the intent of the attack and resists the attack.

17
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:02,000
Next is the verification agent. After the patch has been applied,

18
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:07,700
it will re-attack with the same prompt which caused the breach.

19
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:10,500
Next is the signature.

20
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:12,500
This comes in the audit ledger part.

21
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:18,300
So every event that is performed will be signed, so that we get evidence

22
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:21,200
of what action was performed and what the action did.

23
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:25,200
So this is the target,

24
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:28,300
which is called LedgerPilot. It is basically an accounts payable system

25
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:33,000
where the agent reads the invoice and matches the vendor in the database.

26
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,000
And it also schedules the payment.

27
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:40,100
Since this is autonomous, an attacker can pose a threat where he can make

28
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:43,200
the amount payable to his bank account directly.

29
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:45,800
So let's start with the assessment.

30
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:50,800
The discovery agent has started to discover all the agents and tools.

31
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:55,800
As soon as the discovery agent has discovered all the agents,

32
00:01:55,900 --> 00:02:02,400
the red teaming agent is going to run the prompt injection or prompt-based attacks.

33
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:05,500
For this, I've created a custom dataset.

34
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:12,100
So this is the database where you can find all the vendor information,

35
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:15,500
purchase orders, receipts, and everything.

36
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:21,500
Here you can see the red teaming agent has initiated the attack.

37
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,800
Here you can see all the agents and the tools that have been called.

38
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:39,300
The prompt was able to breach the vulnerable agent system

39
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:41,900
and reached schedule payment.

40
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,700
Here you can see the target API response, and this is the output of each agent.

41
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:53,600
You can see the payment has been scheduled for the attacker's bank account.

42
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:59,200
As soon as the breach is identified, the defender agent is going to

43
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:04,800
autonomously patch the files directly.

44
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:08,100
Next comes the audit ledger.

45
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:13,400
This audit ledger keeps the records of all the events that have happened.

46
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:15,000
This is the Merkle tree.

47
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:21,000
Here you can see each and every attack or event initiated by the agent is signed.

48
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:25,800
So each signature, or each event, is called a log.

49
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:32,500
These multiple logs are combined to form the parent log,

50
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:38,200
and the parent log will eventually modify the Merkle root.

51
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:43,900
So it keeps on modifying the Merkle hash until the events stop being generated.

52
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:50,000
For example, if an attacker has initiated an attack,

53
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:54,500
it will automatically change this Merkle root.

54
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:59,500
The signature is done by hybrid post-quantum cryptographic signers,

55
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:02,400
Ed25519 and ML-DSA-65.

56
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:11,200
It is also co-signed by the TPM 2.0 hardware chip,

57
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:16,200
which has a key built in from manufacturing.

58
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:22,000
For the live deployment URL,

59
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:27,400
I cannot show the TPM signing, because I cannot remotely call this TPM to sign the event.

60
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:31,500
So I have replaced it with software-based signing.

61
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:37,200
In future, I'm going to expand this entire system to support

62
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:44,500
multimodal systems like text, video, images, et cetera.

63
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:47,000
Thank you so much.

